.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt NQSELFTEST 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqselftest
.Nd known answers for every algorithm family
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fn nqselftest void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nqselftest
runs fixed inputs through the functions of every algorithm family
and compares what they produce with answers built into the library, the kind of self-test a cryptographic module runs before it is
used (FIPS 140-3, which takes its requirements from ISO/IEC 19790:2012,
section 7.10):
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
SHA-224 to SHA-512/256, SHA3-224 to SHA3-512, SHAKE128 and SHAKE256 of
.Dq abc ;
.It
KangarooTwelve and its 256-bit form of
.Dq abc ,
and of 9,000 bytes with the customization string
.Ql noqtis
(a tree of two chunks);
.It
HMAC-SHA-256 and HMAC-SHA-512 (RFC 4231, test case 2);
.It
ML-KEM-512, -768 and -1024: a key pair and an encapsulation from fixed
seeds, and the decapsulation;
.It
ML-DSA-44, -65 and -87: a key pair from a fixed seed, the deterministic
signature of a fixed message and its verification;
.It
SLH-DSA-SHA2-128f and SLH-DSA-SHAKE-128f: a key pair from a fixed seed;
.It
HQC-128: a key pair, an encapsulation and the decapsulation;
.It
Ed25519 and ML-DSA-44+Ed25519: a key pair, a deterministic signature and
its verification;
.It
BLAKE2b-512 of
.Dq abc
(RFC 7693, appendix A);
.It
HKDF-SHA-256 (RFC 5869, test case A.1): the extract and the expand;
.It
PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256 (RFC 7914, section 11, one iteration);
.It
Argon2id (RFC 9106, section 5.3: 32 KiB, 3 passes, 4 lanes);
.It
AES-256-GCM (the GCM specification, test case 14) and ChaCha20-Poly1305
(RFC 8439, section 2.8.2): sealing, opening, and opening with a changed
tag, which must fail;
.It
the ChaCha20 stream (RFC 8439, appendix A.2, test vector 1);
.It
AES-SIV (RFC 5297, appendix A.1) and AES-256-GCM-SIV (RFC 8452,
appendix C.2): sealing, opening, and opening a changed ciphertext, which
must fail;
.It
X25519 (RFC 7748, section 5.2, the first vector);
.It
SHA-1 of the two examples of FIPS 180-4
.Pq Dq abc No and the 448-bit message ;
.It
HMAC_DRBG with SHA-256 (NIST SP 800-90A; the NIST ACVP set
hmacDRBG-1.0, test group 14, test 196): instantiation, a reseed and
two requests;
.It
AES key wrap (RFC 3394, section 4.1): wrapping, unwrapping, and
unwrapping a changed text, which must fail;
.It
AES-128-OCB (RFC 7253, appendix A, the second vector) and AES-128-EAX
(Bellare, Rogaway and Wagner, appendix G, the second vector): sealing,
opening, and opening with a changed tag, which must fail;
.It
RSA verification, PKCS#1 v1.5 and PSS with SHA-256 (test 1 of
Wycheproof's rsa_signature_2048_sha256 and rsa_pss_2048_sha256_mgf1_32
sets), and ECDSA P-256 verification with SHA-256 (test 1 of
ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256): the key read, the signature verified, and a
changed signature, which must fail;
.It
ECDH over P-256 (RFC 8448, section 5): the client's public key and its
shared secret with the server's share.
.El
.Pp
That is 25 tests; tests 7 to 24, from BLAKE2b on, each run the inputs
of the published vector named, so their stored answers are also the
SHA-256 of the return codes and the published outputs.
The answers of tests 0 to 4 and 6 were computed by this library when
its test suite had checked the same functions against the NIST ACVP and
KAT vectors, and that of test 5 (HQC-128) with the HQC reference
implementation v5.1.0: they catch a build that differs from those, not
an error the library always made.
.Pp
Not covered: SLH-DSA signing (only its key generation: one SLH-DSA-128f
signature, which is masked, takes longer than the whole self-test),
HQC-192 and HQC-256, and the formats and protocols built on these
algorithms (capsules, OpenPGP, CMS, TLS 1.3).
.Pp
Each family's keys, ciphertexts, signatures, secrets and return codes
are hashed with SHA-256; a decapsulation or verification that disagrees
changes the hash.
In symbols: test k runs its calls in a fixed order; call j returns
rc_j (nothing, for a function that returns nothing) and writes outputs
o_j.
With byte(rc) the low 8 bits of rc in two's complement and
.Sy ||
concatenation:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
D_k = SHA-256(byte(rc_1) || o_1 || byte(rc_2) || o_2 || ...)
test k passes  iff  D_k = A_k        (A_k stored in src/api/kat.h)
.Ed
.Pp
For tests 7 to 24, A_k is the same hash over the return codes and the
outputs the published vector gives (0x00 for
.Dv NQOK ,
0xfd for
.Dv NQEVRFY ) .
The masked decapsulations and signers run as in normal use, so the
self-test needs the random source: without one (a freestanding build
with no
.Xr nqsetrng 3 )
it returns
.Er NQEKAT .
The freestanding build has no AES-GCM, ChaCha20, AES-SIV, AES-GCM-SIV,
X25519, AES key wrap, OCB, EAX, RSA, ECDSA or ECDH, and leaves their
twelve tests out; it runs the Argon2id test only once
.Xr nqsetal 3
has given it an allocator.
It takes about a tenth of a second.
Its buffers are one static block of about 44 KB, wiped after each test,
held under a lock; only Argon2id allocates, 32 KiB through the
.Xr nqsetal 3
hook.
It may be called from several threads at once; one test runs at a
time and the others wait.
The lock is a spin lock, held for one test at a time (well under a
second): a waiting thread spins, using its processor, until the lock is
free.
It is not re-entrant: a random source set with
.Xr nqsetrng 3 ,
which the self-test calls while it holds the lock, must not call
.Fn nqselftest ,
or the call never returns.
The progress and stop hooks
.Pq Xr nqsetprog 3 , Xr nqsetstop 3
are not called during the self-test.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv NQOK
when every answer matches;
.Dv NQEKAT
when one does not.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if (nqselftest() != NQOK) {
  fputs("noqtis: self-test failed, not used\en", stderr);
  exit(1);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-selftest 1 ,
.Xr nqerr 3 ,
.Xr nqsetal 3
